<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Citation Grounding Audit — Eboda Ask</title>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Source+Sans+3:ital,wght@0,300..900;1,300..900&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="eboda-web-toolkit.css">
<style>

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     Additions to the toolkit. Everything interactive here is CSS only —
     hidden radio inputs plus sibling selectors, and <details> for the
     source drawers. No JavaScript anywhere on this page (SPEC.md §8).
     ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  /* verdict pills */
  .verdict { display:inline-block; padding:2px 9px; border-radius:11px;
             font-size:var(--type-body-xxs-size); font-weight:700; white-space:nowrap; }
  .verdict.supported   { background:#E3F3E6; color:#1B6B2E; }
  .verdict.partially   { background:#FFF3D6; color:#8A5A00; }
  .verdict.unsupported { background:#FDE4E4; color:#A31B1B; }
  .verdict.unrelated   { background:#EFE4FD; color:#5B21A3; }
  .verdict.none        { background:var(--color-gray-200); color:var(--color-gray-700); }

  .stat { border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius:10px; padding:var(--spacing-s); }
  .stat .n { font-size:var(--type-heading-xl-size); font-weight:800; line-height:1.05; display:block; }
  .stat .n.win  { color:#1B6B2E; }
  .stat .n.warn { color:#A31B1B; }

  /* ---------------- the Ask modal ---------------- */
  .ask-wrap { position:relative; }
  .probe-radio, .sr { position:absolute; opacity:0; pointer-events:none; }

  .ask-grid { display:grid; gap:var(--spacing-s); grid-template-columns:1fr; }
  @media (min-width:1000px) { .ask-grid { grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; } }

  .ask-shell { background:#fff; border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius:14px;
               box-shadow:0 12px 40px rgba(0,0,0,.10); display:flex; flex-direction:column;
               overflow:hidden; min-height:520px; }
  .ask-bar { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:8px; padding:14px 18px;
             border-bottom:1px solid var(--border-color); }
  .ask-logo { font-weight:900; letter-spacing:-.02em; font-size:15px; }
  .ask-beta { font-size:10px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.08em; color:var(--color-gray-600); }
  .ask-bar .spacer { margin-left:auto; }
  .ask-chev { color:var(--color-gray-500); font-size:13px; }

  .ask-body { padding:18px; flex:1; overflow-y:auto; max-height:560px; }
  .ask-hello { color:var(--color-gray-600); font-size:var(--type-body-s-size);
               margin-bottom:var(--spacing-s); }

  .bubble-user { background:#EDEBFC; border-radius:14px; padding:10px 16px; max-width:82%;
                 margin-left:auto; font-size:var(--type-body-s-size); }

  .answer { margin-top:var(--spacing-s); font-size:var(--type-body-s-size); }
  .line { margin:0 0 6px; }
  .line.plain { color:var(--text-color); white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.55; }

  /* a citable sentence: click to highlight and reveal its source */
  .claim { display:block; cursor:pointer; border-radius:6px; padding:3px 6px; margin:0 -6px;
           border-bottom:2px solid transparent; transition:background .12s, border-color .12s; }
  .claim:hover { background:var(--color-gray-100); }
  .claim .mk { display:inline-block; min-width:16px; text-align:center; margin-left:6px;
               padding:0 4px; border-radius:4px; background:var(--color-gray-200);
               color:var(--color-gray-700); font-size:10px; font-weight:700;
               vertical-align:super; }
  .claim.s-supported   { border-bottom-color:#8FCF9E; }
  .claim.s-partially   { border-bottom-color:#E8C168; }
  .claim.s-unsupported { border-bottom-color:#E79393; }
  .claim.s-unrelated   { border-bottom-color:#B695E8; }
  .claim.s-none        { border-bottom-color:var(--color-gray-300); }

  /* checked state — one generic rule, no per-sentence CSS */
  .sr:checked + .claim { background:#F3F7FF; border-bottom-width:2px; }
  .sr:checked + .claim.s-supported   { background:#EDF8F0; }
  .sr:checked + .claim.s-partially   { background:#FFF8E8; }
  .sr:checked + .claim.s-unsupported { background:#FDEFEF; }
  .sr:checked + .claim.s-unrelated   { background:#F6EFFD; }
  .sr:checked + .claim .mk { background:var(--color-info-accent); color:#fff; }
  .why { display:none; }
  .sr:checked ~ .why { display:block; }

  .why { margin:8px 0 14px; border-left:3px solid var(--color-info-accent);
         background:var(--color-gray-100); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:12px 14px; }
  .why-head { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:8px; margin-bottom:6px; flex-wrap:wrap; }
  .why q { font-style:italic; display:block; margin:6px 0; color:var(--text-color); }
  .why .src-line { font-size:var(--type-body-xxs-size); color:var(--color-gray-700); }
  .why .close { margin-left:auto; cursor:pointer; font-weight:700; color:var(--color-gray-600);
                font-size:13px; padding:0 4px; }
  .why .close:hover { color:var(--text-color); }

  .dropped-note { text-decoration:line-through var(--color-gray-500); opacity:.6; }

  /* product card, mirroring the shipped concierge */
  .prod-card { border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius:10px; padding:14px;
               margin-top:var(--spacing-s); max-width:360px; }
  .prod-mark { width:34px; height:34px; border-radius:7px; background:var(--color-brand-red);
               color:#fff; font-weight:900; display:flex; align-items:center;
               justify-content:center; font-size:15px; }
  .prod-actions { display:flex; gap:8px; margin-top:10px; }

  .react { display:flex; gap:14px; margin-top:12px; color:var(--color-gray-500); font-size:14px; }

  .srcs { margin-top:var(--spacing-s); border-top:1px solid var(--border-color); padding-top:10px; }
  .srcs summary { cursor:pointer; list-style:none; font-weight:700;
                  font-size:var(--type-body-xs-size); }
  .srcs summary::-webkit-details-marker { display:none; }
  .srcs summary::before { content:"›"; display:inline-block; margin-right:7px;
                          transition:transform .15s; }
  .srcs[open] summary::before { transform:rotate(90deg); }
  .srcs ol { margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:22px; }
  .srcs li { margin-bottom:6px; font-size:var(--type-body-xxs-size); }

  /* follow-up chips double as the probe switcher */
  .chips { padding:0 18px 14px; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:8px; }
  .chip { display:block; cursor:pointer; background:var(--color-gray-100);
          border:1px solid transparent; border-radius:10px; padding:9px 14px;
          font-size:var(--type-body-xs-size); transition:background .12s, border-color .12s; }
  .chip:hover { background:var(--color-gray-200); }
  .chip .arr { color:var(--color-gray-500); margin-right:8px; }

  .ask-foot { border-top:1px solid var(--border-color); padding:14px 18px; }
  .ask-input { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:10px; border:1px solid var(--color-gray-400);
               border-radius:22px; padding:12px 18px; color:var(--color-gray-500);
               font-size:var(--type-body-s-size); }
  .ask-input .send { margin-left:auto; }
  .ask-legal { text-align:center; font-size:var(--type-body-xxs-size);
               color:var(--color-gray-600); margin-top:10px; }

  .col-tag { display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:8px; }

  .audit-strip { margin-top:var(--spacing-s); border:1px dashed var(--color-gray-400);
                 border-radius:10px; padding:14px 16px; background:#FCFCFD; }
  .audit-head { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:10px; margin-bottom:4px; flex-wrap:wrap; }
  .cite details { border-bottom:1px dotted var(--border-color); }
  .cite summary { cursor:pointer; list-style:none; padding:7px 0; display:flex; gap:8px;
                  align-items:flex-start; }
  .cite summary::-webkit-details-marker { display:none; }
  .cite summary:hover { background:var(--color-gray-100); }
  .why.static { display:block; margin-top:0; }

  .table-scroll { overflow-x:auto; }
  .table-scroll table { min-width:900px; }

  {% for t in threads %}
  #probe-{{ t.id }}:checked ~ .ask-grid .thread-{{ t.id }} { display:block; }
  #probe-{{ t.id }}:checked ~ .chips .chip[for="probe-{{ t.id }}"] {
    background:#EDEBFC; border-color:#C7C0F5; font-weight:700; }
  {% endfor %}
  .thread { display:none; }

  /* ---- step rail ---- */
  .step-rail { display:none; }
  @media (min-width:1560px) {
    .step-rail { display:block; position:fixed; left:calc(50% - 770px); top:120px; width:150px; z-index:90; }
    .step-rail ol { list-style:none; margin:0; padding:0; display:grid; gap:2px; }
    .step-rail a { display:flex; gap:9px; align-items:baseline; padding:5px 8px; border-radius:6px;
      font-size:12px; line-height:1.3; color:var(--color-gray-600); text-decoration:none;
      border-left:2px solid var(--color-gray-200); }
    .step-rail a:hover { background:var(--color-gray-100); text-decoration:none; color:var(--text-color); }
    .step-rail a .rn { font-weight:700; font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums; color:var(--color-gray-500); }
    .step-rail a.on { color:var(--text-color); font-weight:700; border-left-color:var(--color-brand-red); background:#fff; }
    .step-rail a.on .rn { color:var(--color-brand-red); }
    .step-rail .rail-cap { font-size:10px; letter-spacing:.08em; text-transform:uppercase; font-weight:700;
      color:var(--color-gray-500); margin:0 0 8px 10px; }
  }
  /* ---- identity strip + step chips ---- */
  .ident { font-size:12px; letter-spacing:.04em; text-transform:uppercase; font-weight:700;
    color:var(--color-gray-600); margin:0 0 var(--spacing-xs); }
  .ident span { color:var(--color-gray-400); margin:0 6px; }
  .steps-strip { display:flex; gap:8px; overflow-x:auto; padding-bottom:6px; }
  .steps-strip a { flex:0 0 auto; display:flex; gap:8px; align-items:baseline; text-decoration:none;
    border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius:20px; padding:7px 14px; background:#fff;
    color:var(--text-color); font-size:13px; font-weight:600; }
  .steps-strip a:hover { border-color:var(--color-gray-400); text-decoration:none; background:var(--color-gray-100); }
  .steps-strip a .sn { width:17px; height:17px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--text-color); color:#fff;
    font-size:10px; font-weight:700; display:inline-grid; place-items:center; }
  /* ---- step headings ---- */
  .step-eyebrow { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:10px; margin-bottom:6px; }
  .step-eyebrow .num { width:26px; height:26px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--text-color); color:#fff;
    font-size:13px; font-weight:800; display:grid; place-items:center; flex:0 0 auto; }
  .step-eyebrow .of { font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.09em; text-transform:uppercase; font-weight:700;
    color:var(--color-gray-500); }
  section[id] { scroll-margin-top:80px; }
  /* ---- conditions box ---- */
  .conditions { font-family:var(--fixed-font-family); font-size:12px; line-height:1.9;
    background:var(--color-gray-100); border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius:8px;
    padding:14px 16px; color:var(--color-gray-700); }
  .conditions b { display:inline-block; min-width:120px; color:var(--text-color); font-weight:700; }
  /* ---- evidence chips ---- */
  .ev { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:6px; align-items:center; margin-top:auto; padding-top:var(--spacing-xs); }
  .ev a { font-family:var(--fixed-font-family); font-size:11px; font-weight:700; padding:3px 8px;
    border-radius:5px; background:var(--color-info-accent-light); color:var(--color-info-accent-down);
    text-decoration:none; }
  .ev a:hover { background:var(--color-info-accent); color:#fff; text-decoration:none; }
  /* ---- impact / effort matrix ---- */
  .matrix { display:grid; grid-template-columns:64px repeat(3,1fr); gap:6px; }
  .matrix .lab { font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; text-transform:uppercase; font-weight:700;
    color:var(--color-gray-500); display:flex; align-items:center; }
  .matrix .cell { border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius:8px; min-height:96px; padding:10px;
    background:#fff; }
  .matrix .cell.pick { border-color:var(--color-brand-red); box-shadow:0 0 0 3px #eb100014; }
  .matrix .dot { display:flex; gap:8px; align-items:flex-start; font-size:13px; line-height:1.35;
    font-weight:600; color:var(--text-color); text-decoration:none; }
  .matrix .dot:hover { text-decoration:none; color:#000; }
  .matrix .dot::before { content:""; width:10px; height:10px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--color-info-accent);
    flex:0 0 auto; margin-top:4px; }
  .matrix .cell.pick .dot::before { background:var(--color-brand-red); }
  .matrix .star { color:var(--color-brand-red); font-weight:800; }
  .matrix-list { display:none; }
  @media (max-width:820px) { .matrix { display:none; } .matrix-list { display:block; } }
  /* ---- pipeline ---- */
  .pipe { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:8px; align-items:center; }
  .pipe .node { border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius:8px; padding:8px 12px; background:#fff;
    font-size:13px; font-weight:600; }
  .pipe .node.judge { border-color:var(--color-brand-red); border-width:2px; background:#fff6f5; }
  .pipe .node.ship { background:var(--color-gray-100); font-weight:700; }
  .pipe .arrow { color:var(--color-gray-400); font-weight:700; }
  .pipe-row + .pipe-row { margin-top:var(--spacing-s); }
  .pipe-tag { font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.08em; text-transform:uppercase; font-weight:700;
    color:var(--color-gray-500); margin-bottom:8px; }
  .gate-steps { counter-reset:g; display:grid; gap:var(--spacing-xs); }
  .gate-steps > div { display:flex; gap:12px; }
  .gate-steps > div::before { counter-increment:g; content:counter(g); flex:0 0 auto; width:24px; height:24px;
    border-radius:50%; background:var(--color-gray-200); color:var(--color-gray-700); font-size:12px;
    font-weight:800; display:grid; place-items:center; margin-top:2px; }
  /* ---- before / after ---- */
  .ba { display:grid; gap:var(--spacing-s); grid-template-columns:1fr; }
  @media (min-width:1000px) { .ba { grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; } }
  .ba-card { border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius:12px; background:#fff; padding:16px;
    box-shadow:0 6px 20px rgba(0,0,0,.05); }
  .ba-card .srcs-flat { list-style:none; margin:12px 0 0; padding:12px 0 0; border-top:1px solid var(--border-color);
    display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:6px; }
  .ba-card .srcs-flat li { font-size:11px; color:var(--color-gray-600); border:1px solid var(--border-color);
    border-radius:5px; padding:2px 7px; }
  /* ---- results gate tiles / data room ---- */
  .notice.loud { border-left-width:4px; border-left-color:var(--color-brand-red); background:#fff;
    border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-left:4px solid var(--color-brand-red); padding:var(--spacing-s); }
  .problem { border:2px solid var(--text-color); border-radius:12px; padding:var(--spacing-s); background:#fff; }
  /* ---- audit summary + expander ---- */
  .sumbar { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:8px; }
  .sumbar .chipcount { display:flex; gap:8px; align-items:baseline; border:1px solid var(--border-color);
    border-radius:9px; padding:8px 12px; background:#fff; font-size:12px; color:var(--color-gray-600); }
  .sumbar .chipcount b { font-size:15px; color:var(--text-color); font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums; }
  .audit-x > summary { cursor:pointer; list-style:none; border:1px solid var(--color-gray-400); border-radius:10px;
    padding:14px 18px; background:#fff; display:flex; gap:12px; align-items:baseline; }
  .audit-x > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display:none; }
  .audit-x > summary:hover { background:var(--color-gray-100); }
  .audit-x > summary .chev { margin-left:auto; transition:transform .15s; color:var(--color-gray-500); font-weight:700; }
  .audit-x[open] > summary .chev { transform:rotate(90deg); }
  .filters { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:6px; align-items:center; margin:var(--spacing-s) 0 var(--spacing-xs); }
  .filters button { font-family:inherit; font-size:12px; font-weight:700; cursor:pointer; border-radius:16px;
    border:1px solid var(--color-gray-300); background:#fff; color:var(--color-gray-700); padding:5px 12px; }
  .filters button:hover { border-color:var(--color-gray-500); }
  .filters button.on { background:var(--text-color); border-color:var(--text-color); color:#fff; }
  .filters .live { margin-left:auto; font-size:12px; font-weight:400; color:var(--color-gray-600); }
  .audit-scroll { max-height:65vh; overflow:auto; border:1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius:8px; }
  .audit-scroll table { min-width:900px; font-size:13px; }
  .audit-scroll thead th { position:sticky; top:0; background:#fff; z-index:2; box-shadow:0 1px 0 var(--border-color); }
  .audit-scroll td.clamp { max-width:300px; }
  .audit-scroll tr:not(.open) td.clamp > span { display:-webkit-box; -webkit-line-clamp:2; -webkit-box-orient:vertical;
    overflow:hidden; }
  .audit-scroll tbody tr { cursor:pointer; }
  .audit-scroll tr.hidden { display:none; }
  .collapse-row { display:flex; justify-content:center; padding:var(--spacing-xs) 0 0; }
  .whatnotice p.thread { display:none; border-left:2px solid var(--color-gray-300); padding-left:12px;
    margin:0 0 var(--spacing-s); }
  {% for t in threads %}
  #probe-{{ t.id }}:checked ~ .whatnotice .thread-{{ t.id }} { display:block; }
  {% endfor %}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header class="gnav">
  <div class="gnav-inner">
    <a class="gnav-brand" href="#top"><span class="gnav-mark">E</span> Eboda</a>
    <ul class="gnav-links">
      <li><a href="#why">1. Why hire?</a></li>
      <li><a href="#test">2. Testing</a></li>
      <li><a href="#findings">3. Problems</a></li>
      <li><a href="#prioritize">4. Prioritization</a></li>
      <li><a href="#fix">5. Solution</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ask">6. Validation</a></li>
      <li><a href="#method">Data &amp; method</a></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="gnav-actions"><span class="badge">Prototype</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<nav class="step-rail" aria-label="Steps">
  <p class="rail-cap">The path</p>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#why" data-rail="why"><span class="rn">1</span><span>Why this role exists</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="#test" data-rail="test"><span class="rn">2</span><span>Test the thing</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="#findings" data-rail="findings"><span class="rn">3</span><span>Read the results</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="#prioritize" data-rail="prioritize"><span class="rn">4</span><span>Pick one problem</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="#fix" data-rail="fix"><span class="rn">5</span><span>Build the fix</span></a></li>
    <li><a href="#ask" data-rail="ask"><span class="rn">6</span><span>Check it worked</span></a></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<main id="top">

<!-- ====================== HERO — THE FINDING ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-white" id="numbers">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="ident">A product exercise for Adobe’s Senior PM, adobe.com AI<span>·</span>Tanner Henderson<span>·</span>Measured {{ probed_on }}<span>·</span>Not affiliated with Adobe</p>
    <p class="detail-l">Citation grounding audit</p>
    <h1 class="heading-xxl">Adobe’s Brand Concierge cites {{ n_pages }} pages across {{ n_probes }} prompts. {{ pct(1 - baseline.grounding_rate) }} of its claims aren’t supported by any of them.</h1>
    <p class="body-xl measure mt-m">
      I ran {{ n_probes }} prompts through the Ask beta on adobe.com, kept everything it said and
      every page it cited, then scored each factual sentence against the pages behind it.
      {{ pct(1 - baseline.grounding_rate) }} of what it told me wasn’t in any source it pointed at.
      This page is the whole path from “why would Adobe hire a PM for this?” to a working fix —
      six steps, all the data underneath.
    </p>

    <div class="grid four-up mt-l">
      <div class="stat"><span class="n warn">{{ pct(baseline.grounding_rate) }}</span><p class="detail-m">Adobe claims backed by a page it cited</p><p class="body-xs muted">{{ baseline.supported + baseline.partially }} of {{ baseline.claims }} judged claims</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n win">{{ pct(eboda.grounding_rate) }}</span><p class="detail-m">My version, same corpus</p><p class="body-xs muted">{{ eboda.supported + eboda.partially }} of {{ eboda.claims }} judged sentences</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{{ gate.answers_abstained }} of {{ gate.answers_measured }}</span><p class="detail-m">Answers mine refused to give rather than guess</p><p class="body-xs muted">of the {{ gate.answers_measured }} probes with a corpus to query</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{{ human_agreement_display }}</span><p class="detail-m">Agreement between the AI judge and me</p><p class="body-xs muted">blind sample of {{ n_human_labels }} pairs — <a href="#trust">below my own bar, explained</a></p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="steps-strip mt-l">
      <a href="#why"><span class="sn">1</span>Why this role</a>
      <a href="#test"><span class="sn">2</span>{{ n_probes }} probes</a>
      <a href="#findings"><span class="sn">3</span>Three problems</a>
      <a href="#prioritize"><span class="sn">4</span>Impact vs effort</a>
      <a href="#fix"><span class="sn">5</span>Verification gate</a>
      <a href="#ask"><span class="sn">6</span>Both bots, same questions</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== STEP 1 — WHY I LOOKED HERE ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-gray" id="why">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="step-eyebrow"><span class="num">1</span><span class="of">Step 1 of 6 · Why this role exists</span></div>
    <h2 class="heading-l">Why would Adobe hire a PM for this?</h2>
    <p class="body-l measure mt-xs">I started with the job description, not the product. If Adobe is opening a PM role pointed at adobe.com and AI, something on that site is either costing them money or about to. I wrote down what I thought those things might be before I touched anything, so I couldn’t quietly rewrite the question later to match whatever I found.</p>
    <div class="grid three-up mt-l">
      <div class="card"><div class="card-body"><h3 class="heading-xs">A wrong answer here costs real money</h3><p class="body-xs muted">On adobe.com, a hallucinated price, plan, or commitment term isn’t an embarrassing screenshot. It’s refunds, support volume, and potential regulatory exposure. The blast radius is different than it is on a marketing site.</p></div></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="card-body"><h3 class="heading-xs">The science may be ahead of the product thinking</h3><p class="body-xs muted">The role is specific about RAG, agentic orchestration, prompting, fine-tuning, and model selection. That specificity suggests the capability already exists. What’s missing is someone deciding which customer problems to point it at.</p></div></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="card-body"><h3 class="heading-xs">adobe.com is the proof for what Adobe sells</h3><p class="body-xs muted">Adobe sells AI-powered experience software to other companies. If its own site isn’t a showcase for AEM, Target, and AEP, that weakens the Experience Cloud story. A beta assistant on the homepage is Adobe making exactly that argument in public.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="notice mt-l measure"><p class="body-s"><strong>I bet on the Ask beta.</strong> It’s the newest AI surface on adobe.com, it’s labelled beta, and it’s built on Brand Concierge — Adobe’s own product. If a PM is being hired for this area, it’s most likely for this. Everything after this point is me testing that thing rather than speculating about it.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== STEP 2 — HOW I GOT DATA ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-white" id="test">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="step-eyebrow"><span class="num">2</span><span class="of">Step 2 of 6 · Test the thing</span></div>
    <h2 class="heading-l">I tested it for two hours and wrote down everything</h2>
    <p class="body-l measure mt-xs">I couldn’t reason my way to the problems from outside, so I built a probe set: {{ n_probes }} prompts across {{ probe_stats.categories | length }} categories, each written to test a specific way an assistant like this fails, each tagged with the persona it would hurt. I wrote a hypothesis for every prompt before running it. Then I ran them, logged the full response, every source it cited, and the latency, and marked each one Pass, Partial, or Fail.</p>

    <div class="grid two-up mt-l">
      <div>
        <h3 class="heading-xs">The probe set</h3>
        <table class="data-table mt-xs">
          <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Prompts</th><th>What it’s checking</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {% for c in probe_stats.categories %}
            <tr><td><strong>{{ c.name }}</strong></td><td>{{ c.count }}</td><td class="muted">{{ c.checks }}</td></tr>
            {% endfor %}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <div>
          <h3 class="heading-xs">Conditions</h3>
          <div class="conditions mt-xs">
            <div><b>Date</b>{{ probed_on_long }}</div>
            <div><b>Browser</b>Chrome 152.0.7977.64, incognito</div>
            <div><b>Network</b>Wired, 1 Gbps</div>
            <div><b>Locale</b>Lehi, UT</div>
            <div><b>Entry point</b>adobe.com</div>
            <div><b>Timing</b>DevTools — TTFT = waiting for server response; Full = content download</div>
            <div><b>Latency</b>{{ probe_stats.n_timed_subset }}-prompt timed subset run 3× each; remainder single-run</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="heading-xs">Failure modes across {{ n_probes }} prompts</h3>
          <table class="data-table mt-xs">
            <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {% for mode, n in probe_stats.failure_modes %}
              <tr><td>{{ mode }}</td><td>{{ n }}</td></tr>
              {% endfor %}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid four-up mt-l">
      {% for verdict, n in probe_stats.verdicts %}
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{{ n }}</span><p class="detail-m">{{ verdict }}</p></div>
      {% endfor %}
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{{ "%.2f"|format(probe_stats.ttft_median) }}s</span><p class="detail-m">Median TTFT</p><p class="body-xs muted">{{ "%.2f"|format(probe_stats.full_median) }}s median full response · across all {{ n_probes }} prompts</p></div>
    </div>

    <p class="body-l measure mt-l"><strong>{{ probe_stats.pass_n }} of {{ n_probes }} passed.</strong> That’s the part worth saying out loud — this thing mostly works, and the interesting problems are in the other {{ n_probes - probe_stats.pass_n }} and in what the passes were quietly doing wrong underneath.</p>
    <div class="button-group mt-s"><a class="con-button" href="https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1cU51sa0B0CruG032d20rDwjcXpleIgU9zfFougnz0wM">Full probe log — {{ n_probes }} prompts, responses, sources, latency</a></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== STEP 3 — WHAT THE DATA SAID ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-gray" id="findings">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="step-eyebrow"><span class="num">3</span><span class="of">Step 3 of 6 · Read the results</span></div>
    <h2 class="heading-l">Three problems, in the order I noticed them</h2>
    <p class="body-l measure mt-xs">A verdict of Pass only means the visitor got what they came for. It doesn’t mean the answer was safe. When I went back through the log looking at <em>how</em> the answers were built rather than whether they were right, three patterns showed up.</p>
    <div class="grid three-up mt-l">
      <div class="card"><div class="card-body">
        <h3 class="heading-xs">Finding 1 — Same question, different answer</h3>
        <p class="body-xs muted">Ask the same thing twice and you can get a different answer, a different number of sources, or entirely different sources. B2 (cancellation terms) cited one page on runs 1 and 3 and a different page on run 2. D1 upsold Acrobat Pro on run 1, gave a straight free answer on run 2, and pushed a trial on run 3. A2 returned six sources, then five, then two.</p>
        <p class="body-xs"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> You can’t QA, support, or take responsibility for an answer that isn’t stable.</p>
        <div class="ev"><span class="body-xxs muted">Evidence</span><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="A2">A2</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="B2">B2</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="D1">D1</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="D3">D3</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="F5">F5</a></div>
      </div></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="card-body">
        <h3 class="heading-xs">Finding 2 — The sources don’t support the answer</h3>
        <p class="body-xs muted">Sources are attached at the bottom of the whole response, not to the sentence they came from, and a lot of them are only loosely related. A2 answered a Premiere Pro question citing Premiere Rush, Premiere Elements and After Effects pages. B5 answered a renewal-pricing question with a generic pricing page — the actual policy was one more click into the terms. H3 sent a university buyer to a generic special-offers page when a university page exists.</p>
        <p class="body-xs"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> A citation nobody can check is decoration, not evidence.</p>
        <div class="ev"><span class="body-xxs muted">Evidence</span><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="A2">A2</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="B5">B5</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="H3">H3</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="E3">E3</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="I2">I2</a></div>
      </div></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="card-body">
        <h3 class="heading-xs">Finding 3 — The guardrail can’t tell a competitor question from a purchase question</h3>
        <p class="body-xs muted">“What else is out there besides Adobe?” and “Why should I choose Adobe over other options?” both hit the same refusal. The second one is Adobe being asked for its own pitch. Meanwhile “I’m switching from Canva” — which names a competitor — answered fine.</p>
        <p class="body-xs"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> The refusal fires on the word, not the intent, and it’s firing on people who are trying to buy.</p>
        <div class="ev"><span class="body-xxs muted">Evidence</span><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="F1">F1</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="F2">F2</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="F3">F3</a><a href="#audit" data-probe-link="F5">F5</a></div>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="body-xs muted mt-s">Evidence chips open the full audit table filtered to that probe.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== STEP 4 — PICKING ONE ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-white" id="prioritize">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="step-eyebrow"><span class="num">4</span><span class="of">Step 4 of 6 · Pick one problem</span></div>
    <h2 class="heading-l">Impact against effort, and the one I could actually finish</h2>
    <p class="body-l measure mt-xs">Three real problems, one of me, and a job posting with a close date. I plotted them on impact against effort and picked the one where I could put something working in front of you rather than describe it.</p>
    <div class="matrix mt-l">
      <div class="lab"></div><div class="lab">Low effort</div><div class="lab">Med effort</div><div class="lab">High effort</div>
      <div class="lab">High impact</div>
      <div class="cell pick"><a class="dot" href="#findings">Sources don’t support the answer <span class="star">★ chosen</span></a></div>
      <div class="cell"></div>
      <div class="cell"><a class="dot" href="#findings">Same question, different answer</a></div>
      <div class="lab">Med impact</div><div class="cell"></div><div class="cell"></div><div class="cell"></div>
      <div class="lab">Low impact</div>
      <div class="cell"><a class="dot" href="#findings">Guardrail too blunt</a></div>
      <div class="cell"></div><div class="cell"></div>
    </div>
    <ol class="matrix-list body-s mt-s">
      <li><strong>High impact, low effort — sources don’t support the answer.</strong> Chosen.</li>
      <li><strong>High impact, high effort — same question, different answer.</strong> Needs someone inside retrieval.</li>
      <li><strong>Low impact, low effort — guardrail too blunt.</strong> Worth fixing, not worth leading with.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="notice mt-l measure"><p class="body-s"><strong>Why not the bigger one.</strong> Non-determinism is the higher-impact problem and I’d want it on the roadmap. But fixing it means getting into retrieval, temperature, and caching inside a system I can’t see. Grounding I can fix from the outside, and a fix that ships beats a fix that’s described.</p></div>
    <div class="problem mt-l measure"><p class="detail-m">The problem</p><p class="body-l mt-xxs">Sources are generic and don’t always directly support the response. Best case, this costs users confidence in the answers. Worst case, the AI is misusing sources and giving incorrect information, leading to misinformed decisions and unhappy customers.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== STEP 5 — THE FIX ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-gray" id="fix">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="step-eyebrow"><span class="num">5</span><span class="of">Step 5 of 6 · Build the fix</span></div>
    <h2 class="heading-l">A verification gate, and citations you can check</h2>
    <p class="body-l measure mt-xs">The fix is two things that only work together. One is a step in the pipeline that checks each sentence against the source it claims. The other is an interface change that puts the evidence where the reader can see it. Either one alone doesn’t do much — a checker with nothing visible is invisible, and inline citations nobody verified are just tidier decoration.</p>

    <h3 class="heading-s mt-l">5a · The workflow</h3>
    <div class="mt-s">
      <div class="pipe-row">
        <p class="pipe-tag">Adobe, as shipped</p>
        <div class="pipe">
          <span class="node">Question</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="node">Retrieve</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="node">Generate</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="node">Attach sources to the whole answer</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="node ship">Ship</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipe-row">
        <p class="pipe-tag">Mine</p>
        <div class="pipe">
          <span class="node">Question</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="node">Retrieve</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="node">Generate, one citation per sentence</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="node judge">Judge — each sentence vs its own source</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="node">Keep / drop / abstain</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="node ship">Ship</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="gate-steps mt-l">
      <div><p class="body-s"><strong>Generate with sentence-level citations.</strong> Every factual sentence has to name the chunk it came from. If it can’t, it doesn’t get written.</p></div>
      <div><p class="body-s"><strong>Judge each sentence against its own source.</strong> Same rubric as the audit — Supported, Partially supported, Unsupported, Source unrelated. The judge never sees the question, the URL, or the brand. Only the sentence and the source text. That’s deliberate: it stops the model reasoning about whether something <em>sounds</em> true instead of whether the page says it.</p></div>
      <div><p class="body-s"><strong>Drop what fails.</strong> Unsupported and Source unrelated sentences get cut before the answer is returned.</p></div>
      <div><p class="body-s"><strong>The rule that is actually the product.</strong> If a dropped sentence contains a number, a price, or a percentage, the whole answer is withheld. A half-correct pricing answer is worse than no pricing answer.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="notice mt-l measure"><p class="body-s"><strong>When it withholds, it doesn’t just refuse.</strong> It says plainly that it doesn’t have that documented, names the closest thing it does have, and offers a handoff. Compare A9 and A10 in the demo below: Adobe’s version is a dead end, mine is a next step.</p></div>

    <h3 class="heading-s mt-l">5b · The interface</h3>
    <div class="ba mt-s">
      <div class="ba-card">
        <div class="col-tag"><strong class="detail-m">Before</strong><span class="badge">response-level citation</span></div>
        <p class="line plain body-s">Premiere Pro on Windows requires Windows 10 (22H2) or later, 16 GB of RAM, and a GPU with 2 GB of VRAM. Storage requirements depend on the media you work with. You can install it from the Creative Cloud desktop app.</p>
        <ul class="srcs-flat"><li>1. Premiere Rush</li><li>2. Premiere Elements</li><li>3. After Effects</li><li>4. Creative Cloud</li><li>5. Pricing</li><li>6. Support</li></ul>
        <p class="body-xxs muted mt-xs">One list for the whole answer. Nothing ties a sentence to a page — and nothing lets a reader check.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ba-card">
        <div class="col-tag"><strong class="detail-m">After</strong><span class="badge solid">sentence-level citation</span></div>
        <div class="body-s">
          <p class="line"><span class="claim s-supported">Premiere Pro on Windows requires Windows 10 (22H2) or later.<span class="mk">1</span></span></p>
          <p class="line"><span class="claim s-partially">16 GB of RAM is recommended for HD media.<span class="mk">2</span></span></p>
          <p class="line"><span class="claim s-none dropped-note">A GPU with 2 GB of VRAM is required.<span class="mk">–</span></span></p>
        </div>
        <div class="why static mt-xs">
          <div class="why-head"><span class="verdict supported">Supported</span><span class="body-xxs muted">marker 1</span></div>
          <q>Windows 10 (64-bit) V22H2 or later</q>
          <p class="src-line">The page states the minimum Windows build verbatim.</p>
          <p class="src-line">premiere-pro tech specs · chunk 14</p>
        </div>
        <p class="body-xxs muted mt-xs">Every marker points at one passage, and the third sentence never reaches the reader.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="body-l measure mt-s">Adobe cites at the response level — one list of links for the whole answer, so you can’t tell which link backs which sentence, and neither can they. I cite at the sentence level and let you open the exact text I’m relying on. It’s a smaller change than the gate, but it’s the part a customer feels: the confidence comes from being able to check, not from being told to trust.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== STEP 6 — PROOF ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-white" id="ask">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="step-eyebrow"><span class="num">6</span><span class="of">Step 6 of 6 · Check it worked</span></div>
    <h2 class="heading-l">Same six questions, both assistants</h2>
    <p class="body-l measure mt-xs">Same six questions, both assistants, same set of pages underneath. Adobe’s answers are exactly what it gave me on {{ probed_on }}. Mine were generated from the pages Adobe’s own retriever surfaced — I didn’t get to pick a friendlier corpus. <strong>Click any underlined sentence</strong> to see the source text behind it and the judge’s verdict.</p>

    <div class="ask-wrap mt-l">
      {% for t in threads %}
      <input type="radio" name="probe" id="probe-{{ t.id }}" class="probe-radio"{% if loop.first %} checked{% endif %}>
      {% endfor %}

      <div class="whatnotice">
        {% for t in threads %}
        <p class="thread thread-{{ t.id }} body-xs muted"><strong class="detail-s">What to notice</strong> · {{ t.notice }}</p>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>

      <div class="ask-grid">

        <!-- ============ ADOBE COLUMN ============ -->
        <div>
          <div class="col-tag">
            <strong class="detail-m">Adobe Brand Concierge</strong>
            <span class="badge">as shipped</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ask-shell">
            <div class="ask-bar">
              <span class="ask-logo">Ask</span><span class="ask-beta">BETA</span>
              <span class="spacer"></span><span class="ask-chev">⌄</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ask-body">
              <p class="ask-hello">Welcome! Thank you for trying out this initial release.
                The experience is still evolving and will continue to improve over time.</p>

              {% for t in threads %}
              <div class="thread thread-{{ t.id }}">
                <div class="bubble-user">{{ t.prompt }}</div>
                <div class="answer">
                  <p class="line plain">{{ t.adobe_plain }}</p>
                </div>

                {% if t.adobe_sources %}
                <details class="srcs">
                  <summary>Sources ({{ t.adobe_sources | length }})</summary>
                  <ol>
                    {% for s in t.adobe_sources %}
                    <li><a href="{{ s.url }}">{{ s.title }}</a></li>
                    {% endfor %}
                  </ol>
                </details>
                {% else %}
                <div class="srcs"><p class="body-xxs muted">No sources cited in this response.</p></div>
                {% endif %}
                <div class="react"><span>👍</span><span>👎</span></div>
              </div>
              {% endfor %}
            </div>

            <div class="ask-foot">
              <div class="ask-input"><span>Ask a question</span><span class="send">➤</span></div>
              <p class="ask-legal">AI responses may be inaccurate and any offers provided are non-binding.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Not part of Adobe's interface. This is our audit of the answer
               above, which the shipped product gives the user no way to do. -->
          {% for t in threads %}
          <div class="thread thread-{{ t.id }} audit-strip">
            <div class="audit-head">
              <span class="detail-m">Our audit of this answer</span>
              {% if t.adobe_counts.total %}
              <span class="badge">{{ t.adobe_counts.grounded }} of {{ t.adobe_counts.total }} claims grounded</span>
              {% else %}
              <span class="badge">no factual claims</span>
              {% endif %}
            </div>
            {% if t.adobe_counts.total %}
            <p class="body-xxs muted">Adobe's UI offers no way to check any of this. Expand a claim
              to see what the judge found.</p>
            <div class="cite mt-xs">
              {% for c in t.adobe_claims %}
              <details>
                <summary><span class="verdict {{ c.best_slug }}">{{ c.best or 'no source' }}</span>
                  <span class="body-xs">{{ c.text }}</span></summary>
                <div class="why static">
                  <div class="why-head">
                    <span class="body-xxs muted">best of {{ c.sources | length }} cited source{{ 's' if c.sources | length != 1 }}</span>
                  </div>
                  {% if c.best_source %}
                    {% if c.best_source.evidence_span %}
                  <q>{{ c.best_source.evidence_span }}</q>
                    {% endif %}
                  <p class="src-line">{{ c.best_source.reason }}</p>
                  <p class="src-line"><a href="{{ c.best_source.url }}">{{ c.best_source.title }}</a></p>
                  {% endif %}
                  {% if not c.marker_derived and c.sources | length > 1 %}
                  <p class="src-line mt-xxs"><strong>Cited at response level.</strong> This sentence
                    inherits all {{ c.sources | length }} of the answer's sources — nothing in the
                    product ties it to one page.</p>
                  {% endif %}
                </div>
              </details>
              {% endfor %}
            </div>
            {% else %}
            <p class="body-xxs muted">This answer asserted nothing verifiable, so there was nothing
              to score.</p>
            {% endif %}
          </div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>

        <!-- ============ EBODA COLUMN ============ -->
        <div>
          <div class="col-tag">
            <strong class="detail-m">Eboda Ask</strong>
            <span class="badge solid">sentence-verified</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ask-shell">
            <div class="ask-bar">
              <span class="ask-logo">Eboda Ask</span><span class="ask-beta">v2</span>
              <span class="spacer"></span><span class="ask-chev">⌄</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ask-body">
              <p class="ask-hello">Every sentence carries its own source. Anything the verifier
                can't confirm is dropped before you see it.</p>

              {% for t in threads %}
              <div class="thread thread-{{ t.id }}">
                <div class="bubble-user">{{ t.prompt }}</div>

                {% if not t.eboda_present %}
                <div class="answer"><p class="line plain body-s muted">Not run — this probe returned
                  no citations from Adobe, so there was no corpus query to build.</p></div>
                {% else %}
                <div class="answer">
                  {% if t.eboda_abstained %}
                  <p class="line plain">{{ t.eboda_final }}</p>
                  <p class="body-xxs muted mt-xs"><span class="verdict none">withheld</span>
                    {{ t.eboda_reason }}</p>
                  {% endif %}

                  {% for s in t.eboda_sentences %}
                  <div class="line">
                    <input type="radio" name="{{ t.id }}-e" id="{{ t.id }}-e-{{ s.n }}" class="sr">
                    <label for="{{ t.id }}-e-{{ s.n }}" class="claim s-{{ s.slug }}"><span{% if not s.kept %} class="dropped-note"{% endif %}>{{ s.text }}</span><span class="mk">{{ s.marks | join(' ') if s.marks else '–' }}</span></label>
                    <div class="why">
                      <div class="why-head">
                        <span class="verdict {{ s.slug }}">{{ s.label or 'no citation' }}</span>
                        <span class="body-xxs muted">{{ 'kept' if s.kept else 'dropped by the gate' }}</span>
                        <label for="{{ t.id }}-e-clear" class="close" title="close">✕</label>
                      </div>
                      {% if s.sources %}
                        {% for src in s.sources %}
                          {% if src.evidence_span %}
                      <q>{{ src.evidence_span }}</q>
                          {% endif %}
                      <p class="src-line">{{ src.reason }}</p>
                      <p class="src-line"><a href="{{ src.url }}">{{ src.title }}</a> · {{ src.chunk_id }}</p>
                        {% endfor %}
                      {% else %}
                      <p class="src-line">{{ s.reason or 'Cited no retrievable source, so it could not be verified.' }}</p>
                      {% endif %}
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  {% endfor %}
                  <input type="radio" name="{{ t.id }}-e" id="{{ t.id }}-e-clear" class="sr" checked>
                </div>

                {% if t.eboda_sources %}
                <details class="srcs">
                  <summary>Sources ({{ t.eboda_sources | length }})</summary>
                  <ol>
                    {% for s in t.eboda_sources %}
                    <li><a href="{{ s.url }}">{{ s.title }}</a>{% if s.heading %} — {{ s.heading }}{% endif %}</li>
                    {% endfor %}
                  </ol>
                </details>
                {% endif %}
                <div class="react"><span>👍</span><span>👎</span></div>
                {% endif %}
              </div>
              {% endfor %}
            </div>

            <div class="ask-foot">
              <div class="ask-input"><span>Ask a question</span><span class="send">➤</span></div>
              <p class="ask-legal">Eboda cites at the sentence level and drops claims it can't verify.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="chips mt-s">
        <p class="detail-m">Try another question</p>
        {% for t in threads %}
        <label class="chip" for="probe-{{ t.id }}"><span class="arr">↳</span>{{ t.prompt }}
          <span class="body-xxs muted"> · {{ t.id }} · {{ t.category }}</span></label>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="notice mt-l measure">
      <p class="body-s"><strong>The difference in one line.</strong> Adobe answers, then lists its
      sources at the bottom of the response. Nothing connects a given sentence to a given page, and
      the interface gives you no way to check one — so the audit strip under its answer is ours, not
      a feature of the product. Eboda cites per sentence: every marker points at one passage, the
      underline colour is the judge's verdict on that exact pairing, and anything the verifier
      couldn't confirm never reaches the user.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== RESULTS ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-gray" id="results">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="detail-l">Results</p>
    <h2 class="heading-l">What the two systems produced</h2>
    <div class="grid two-up mt-l">
      <div>
        <h3 class="heading-xs">Where Adobe’s claims land</h3>
        <table class="data-table mt-xs">
          <thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th>Claims</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict supported">Supported</span></td><td>{{ baseline.supported }}</td><td>{{ pct(baseline.supported / baseline.claims) }}</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict partially">Partially supported</span></td><td>{{ baseline.partially }}</td><td>{{ pct(baseline.partially / baseline.claims) }}</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict unsupported">Unsupported</span></td><td>{{ baseline.unsupported }}</td><td>{{ pct(baseline.unsupported / baseline.claims) }}</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict unrelated">Source unrelated</span></td><td>{{ baseline.unrelated }}</td><td>{{ pct(baseline.unrelated / baseline.claims) }}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="heading-xs">Where Eboda’s sentences land</h3>
        <table class="data-table mt-xs">
          <thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th>Sentences</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict supported">Supported</span></td><td>{{ eboda.supported }}</td><td>{{ pct(eboda.supported / eboda.claims) }}</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict partially">Partially supported</span></td><td>{{ eboda.partially }}</td><td>{{ pct(eboda.partially / eboda.claims) }}</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict unsupported">Unsupported</span></td><td>{{ eboda.unsupported }}</td><td>{{ pct(eboda.unsupported / eboda.claims) }}</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict unrelated">Source unrelated</span></td><td>{{ eboda.unrelated }}</td><td>{{ pct(eboda.unrelated / eboda.claims) }}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid three-up mt-l">
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{{ gate.answers_abstained }} of {{ gate.answers_measured }}</span><p class="detail-m">Answers withheld by the gate</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{{ gate.sentences_generated }}</span><p class="detail-m">Sentences generated</p><p class="body-xs muted">{{ gate.sentences_kept }} reached a reader</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{{ gate.sentences_dropped }}</span><p class="detail-m">Sentences dropped before shipping</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="notice loud mt-l"><p class="body-s"><strong>Read the second number carefully.</strong> {{ pct(baseline.grounding_rate) }} and {{ pct(eboda.grounding_rate) }} aren’t measuring identical things. Adobe cites at the response level, so every claim inherits every source and I score it against the best match — the most charitable reading available. Mine cites at the sentence level, so each sentence is judged against the one chunk it named, and anything that failed was removed before it counted. The honest framing isn’t “mine is {{ ((eboda.grounding_rate - baseline.grounding_rate) * 100) | round | int }} points better.” It’s that a verification gate converts wrong answers into withheld answers, and that trade is the whole design.</p></div>
    <p class="body-l measure mt-l">Withholding half the answers is not a bug in this build. It’s what the corpus can actually support. The number to watch in production isn’t the grounding rate, it’s how often we withhold — because that’s a content gap telling you which page to go write.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== HOW I CHECKED MYSELF ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-white" id="trust">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="detail-l">Checking myself</p>
    <h2 class="heading-l">How I checked the judge</h2>
    <div class="grid two-up mt-s">
      <div class="stack">
        <p class="body-l">An AI grading an AI is only worth something if somebody checked the grader. So the pipeline pulled {{ n_human_labels }} random claim–source pairs (fixed seed, so it’s reproducible), stripped the model’s labels out of the file so I wouldn’t be anchored, and I labelled all {{ n_human_labels }} by hand against the source text. Then I compared.</p>
        <p class="body-l"><strong>We agreed on {{ human_agreement_display }} of them.</strong> I’d set {{ "%.2f"|format(agreement_threshold) }} as the bar before I ran it, and I want to be straight about missing it rather than quietly dropping the number.</p>
        <p class="body-s">Where we disagreed was almost entirely the line between <em>Partially supported</em> and <em>Unsupported</em> — cases like A2 on the After Effects page, where a Windows 11 requirement is on the page but for a different product. The model read subject-area overlap as partial support; I read a different product as no support. Supported and Source unrelated we agreed on. So the disagreement is on the middle of the scale, not the ends, which means the headline direction holds and the precise {{ pct(baseline.grounding_rate) }} has a real error bar on it.</p>
        <p class="body-s">If I ran this again: tighten that rubric line with worked examples, take the sample to 50, and get a second human on it. That’s a rubric fix, not a code fix — and it’s the kind of thing I’d rather show you than paper over.</p>
        <div class="button-group"><a class="con-button" href="https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1S0R1PODBiwF-w-tLkrq25PPjftTrkXDLQJYq15UQMHA">The {{ n_human_labels }} hand-labelled pairs</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <div class="stat"><span class="n">{{ human_agreement_display }}</span><p class="detail-m">Exact-match agreement</p><p class="body-xs muted">{{ n_human_labels }} pairs · bar set at {{ "%.2f"|format(agreement_threshold) }}{% if binary_agreement_display %} · {{ binary_agreement_display }} on the binary grounded / not-grounded call the headline depends on{% endif %}</p></div>
        <div class="notice"><p class="body-xs">The judge sees only the claim text and the source text — never the URL, the question, or the brand.</p></div>
        <div class="notice"><p class="body-xs">Sources that couldn’t be fetched are their own bucket and excluded from the headline rate.</p></div>
        <div class="notice"><p class="body-xs"><code>grounding_rate = (Supported + Partially supported) / judged claims</code>, defined once, in code.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== FULL AUDIT — COLLAPSED BY DEFAULT ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-gray" id="audit">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="detail-l">Every judgement</p>
    <h2 class="heading-l">The full audit</h2>
    <p class="body-l measure mt-xs">Every claim–source pair both systems produced, with the judge’s verdict and its reason. Nothing is filtered, nothing is cherry-picked. If a number on this page is wrong, it’s wrong here first.</p>

    <div class="sumbar mt-s">
      <div class="chipcount"><b>{{ audit_summary.adobe }}</b> Adobe pairs</div>
      <div class="chipcount"><b>{{ audit_summary.eboda }}</b> Eboda pairs</div>
      {% for v in audit_summary.verdicts %}
      <div class="chipcount"><span class="verdict {{ v.slug }}">{{ v.label }}</span><b>{{ v.n }}</b> {{ pct(v.share) }}</div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>

    <details class="audit-x mt-s">
      <summary>
        <span>
          <strong class="heading-xs">Open the full audit — {{ audit_summary.total }} claim–source pairs</strong>
          <span class="body-xs muted" style="display:block">Claim, source, verdict, and the judge’s reason for each. Long — most people should read the summary above and spot-check a few rows.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="chev">›</span>
      </summary>

      <div class="filters">
        <button type="button" data-filter="all" class="on">All</button>
        <button type="button" data-filter="sys:adobe">Adobe</button>
        <button type="button" data-filter="sys:eboda">Eboda</button>
        {% for v in audit_summary.verdicts %}
        <button type="button" data-filter="v:{{ v.slug }}">{{ v.label }}</button>
        {% endfor %}
        <span class="live" data-live>{{ audit_summary.total }} of {{ audit_summary.total }} rows</span>
      </div>

      <div class="audit-scroll">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Claim ID</th><th>System</th><th>Claim</th><th>Source</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody data-audit-body>
            {% for row in table %}
            <tr data-sys="{{ row.system | lower }}" data-verdict="{{ row.slug }}" data-probe="{{ row.probe_id }}">
              <td>{{ row.claim_id }}</td>
              <td>{{ row.system }}</td>
              <td class="clamp"><span>{{ row.claim }}</span></td>
              <td>{% if row.source_url %}<a href="{{ row.source_url }}">{{ row.source }}</a>{% else %}{{ row.source }}{% endif %}</td>
              <td><span class="verdict {{ row.slug }}">{{ row.label }}</span></td>
              <td class="clamp"><span>{{ row.reason }}</span></td>
            </tr>
            {% endfor %}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="collapse-row">
        <button type="button" class="con-button" data-collapse-audit>Collapse table ▴</button>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ====================== METHOD, LIMITS, WHAT'S NEXT ====================== -->
<section class="section bg-white" id="method">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="detail-l">Method</p>
    <h2 class="heading-l">How these numbers were produced</h2>

    <div class="grid three-up mt-l">
      <div class="stack">
        <h3 class="heading-xs">Method</h3>
        <p class="body-xs muted">{{ n_probes }} prompts run {{ probed_on }}, judged {{ measured_at }}. Responses and cited URLs taken verbatim from the log. Pages fetched and extracted, then chunked. Retrieval is BM25 — no embeddings, no vector store, on purpose: I wanted the measurement to be the interesting part, not the stack. Temperature 0 everywhere, every call cached, so the run is reproducible.</p>
        <table class="data-table">
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>Probes run</th><td>{{ n_probes }} ({{ n_probes_cited }} returned citations)</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Claims extracted</th><td>{{ n_claims_total }} factual sentences</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Claim–source pairs judged</th><td>{{ audit_summary.total }}</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Pages fetched</th><td>{{ n_pages }}, chunked into {{ n_chunks }} passages</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Judge model</th><td>{{ judge_model }}</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Generation model</th><td>{{ generation_model }}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="stack">
        <h3 class="heading-xs">What this doesn’t show</h3>
        <ul class="body-xs muted stack-s">
          <li>The corpus is only the pages Adobe’s own retriever surfaced. This is not a test of site-wide search.</li>
          <li>Most probes ran once. Only the {{ probe_stats.n_timed_subset }}-prompt timed subset ran three times, which is where the non-determinism finding comes from — a fuller test would run all {{ n_probes }}.</li>
          <li>Human agreement is {{ human_agreement_display }} on {{ n_human_labels }} pairs. See <a href="#trust">how I checked the judge</a>.</li>
          <li>{{ n_claims_uncited }} extracted claims carried no citation at all, so there was nothing to judge them against.</li>
          <li>I’m outside the company. I can see the output, not the system, the roadmap, or the constraints behind either.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="stack">
        <h3 class="heading-xs">What I’d do with a team</h3>
        <ul class="body-xs muted stack-s">
          <li>Ship the gate behind a flag on pricing and terms questions only — the highest-risk, smallest surface.</li>
          <li>Instrument withholding rate by category. Every withheld answer is a content gap with an owner.</li>
          <li>Take the non-determinism finding to whoever owns retrieval; it needs someone inside.</li>
          <li>Success metric isn’t grounding rate. It’s support contacts and refunds tied to assistant sessions.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="body-xxs muted mt-l">The Eboda styling is a visual shell for a portfolio prototype. The content and product names underneath are real Adobe pages. Not affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to Adobe.</p>
  </div>
</section>

